# setOutputVolume() now sets every channel to the same value

Reviewed head: `1e703c922d902f1c70d68931da778d5a86a291d7` (merge base `b679363bed05415771a1b1dc92c6899a908236f7`).

`setOutputVolume()` now runs `pactl set-sink-volume <sink> <pct>%` with one value. pactl applies a single value to every channel (`fill_volume()` in `src/utils/pactl.c`, PulseAudio 17.0, lines 1814 to 1829: with one value it calls `pa_cvolume_set()` for all supported channels). Quickshell's `audio.volume` setter scales the channels in proportion instead (`PwNodeBoundAudio::setAverageVolume`, `src/services/pipewire/node.cpp`, Quickshell 0.3.1).

## Runtime check

Headless Quickshell 0.3.1 (Arch package, `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen`) against private PipeWire 1.6.8 and pipewire-pulse servers with one stereo null sink. The harness copies the `sink`/`volumeSink`/`outputVolume` declarations, `setOutputVolume()` and the `omarchy-audio-output-sink` process verbatim from each revision's `Panel.qml`. Each trial sets an unequal balance with `pactl set-sink-volume <sink> L% R%`, waits for Quickshell to report it, calls `setOutputVolume(target)` over Quickshell IPC, and reads the channels with `pactl --format=json list sinks` 2 s later. Each row ran twice with the same result.

| Start (front-left / front-right) | `setOutputVolume()` argument | merge base `b679363b` result | head `1e703c92` result |
|---|---|---|---|
| 40% / 60% | 0.6 | 48% / 72% | 60% / 60% |
| 30% / 70% | 0.3 | 18% / 42% | 30% / 30% |

The same path runs for the output slider (`onMoved`), the bar icon's wheel and the panel's `h`/`l` keys, on every sink type. The volume keys (`omarchy-audio-output-volume`) already use a single-value `pactl set-sink-volume`, so they flatten the balance too.

## Manual check on a live system (not run for this review)

1. `sink=$(omarchy-audio-output-sink); pactl set-sink-volume "$sink" 40% 60%`
2. Move the output slider in the audio panel.
3. `pactl get-sink-volume "$sink"` shows whether the two channels are still different.
</details>

</details>

<details><summary>Details</summary>

**Verified:**
- Quickshell 0.3.1 `PwNodeBoundAudio::setVolumes` sends a device-route volume write only when `volumeStep != -1` (`src/services/pipewire/node.cpp`), so the pactl volume call should reach the affected Bluetooth sinks (source-verified, not tested on hardware). BlueZ publishes `volumeStep` only when the transport's hardware volume is active, so the affected outputs are Bluetooth sinks without it, not every BlueZ sink.
- pipewire-pulse writes the card route for `set-sink-volume` without a volume-step check. Not tested on Bluetooth hardware.
- `toggleAllMuted()` with an explicit `1`/`0` set the expected state in 10 of 10 calls at the head, and its input handling is unchanged.
- `test/shell.d/audio-test.sh` passes at the head (20 `ok` lines); it loads only `Model.js`, so the changed QML functions have no automated coverage.

**Mute root cause:** Quickshell 0.3.1 `setMuted` has no `volumeStep` gate and BlueZ applies route mute, so the stated root cause does not explain a Bluetooth mute failure; the pactl mute calls may be unnecessary.

**Related:** upstream Quickshell commit `25da30d` ("pipewire: write route volumes without volume step", not yet in a release) removes the gate. Open PRs #13004 and #13099 change `setOutputVolume()` for the same issue, #12886, with a single serialized pactl writer instead of one detached process per slider event.
</details>

